Deep Learning: What It Is and how it works
//In recent years, deep learning has reshaped everything from natural language processing to autonomous vehicles. 
//But what is it really? How does it differ from traditional machine learning? And what makes it work under the hood?

This article aims to give a concise yet comprehensive introduction to deep learning, including how training works, how 
weights are computed and initialized, what activation functions do, and how modern techniques like batch normalization and 
dropout help overcome fundamental challenges.

Without the activation function  even the deepest network is only as good as a single neuron.
Activation function make the learning of non linear patterns possible.

At the output layer use the sigmoid for classification problem

weights and biases are updated so that the prediction error can decrease.
In backpropagation the goal is for each of the weights and biases how much they are contributing to the error 
backpropagation calculates the relationship between the parameter and the error.

Gradient vector gathers how the network's parameters relate to the cost 

Through backpropagation we learn how much weights and biases affect the outcome
Optimization algorithm decides in which direction to go to change weights.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why go deeper?
More layers allow the model to learn more abstract, complex patterns.
Each ReLU layer introduces non-linearity, letting the network bend the decision boundary more flexibly.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🤖 Deep Learning vs Machine Learning
While machine learning broadly refers to systems that learn from data to make decisions, deep learning is a subset that
 uses neural networks with many layers (hence “deep”).

Machine Learning	Deep Learning
Works with structured data	Excels with unstructured data (images, audio, text)
Manual feature engineering	Learns features automatically
Algorithms like decision trees, SVMs	Neural networks with hidden layers

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The most basic building block of a neural network is the Neuron (also called a "unit" or "node")

A neuron is a function that takes weighted inputs, Adds a bias and applies an activation function (e.g., ReLU, sigmoid)

y = f(Σ(wᵢxᵢ) + b)
where xᵢ are the inputs, wᵢ are the weights, b is the bias and f is the activation function.

🏗 From Neuron to Network: 
With some Neuron → the smallest unit
it is possible to create a Layer → a collection of neurons
By stacking multiple layer on top of each other we can create a Network → layers stacked together

