OPTIMIZATION ALGORITHMS
 Optimization algorithms are core to training deep learning models — they determine how the model learns from data by updating weights to minimize a loss function. Here's a structured overview of the most important optimization algorithms used in deep learning:
 --------------
-Gradient descent
Updates weights using the gradient of the loss function:
Batch Gradient Descent: Uses the whole dataset to compute gradients.
It doesn't care about previous values, its speed changes 
as it moves thorugh the function's domain.
Change = learnRate *  [grads]

✅ Stable, deterministic

❌ Slow for large datasets
--------------
-Gradient descent with momentum
Adds a memory of past updates to accelerate convergence:
 Smooths out updates

 Change = beta * changeFromPreivousStep - learnRate *  [grads]

✅ Escapes small local minima faster.
It uses previous gradients.
Nearly always converges faster than GD
It lowers oscillations
goes thoruhg plateaus areas very fast, because it remembers how fast it went before.
--------------
-Stocasthic Gradient descent 
Uses a single data point (or small batch) to update weights:
MINI BATCHES
-Batch GD: Run the whole data through the network. since it can see all the data, the path to the optimum is more stright. One iteration takes longer because all of the data are considered.
-Mini-batch GD. Run 2 or more  but less than the  whole dta through the network.
Since it doesn't see all the data, the path to the optimum will be more "squiggly", the minibatches might represents something a little bit "off" respect to the whole data. mini batches sizes should be powers of 2
-Stochastic GD: run examples one by one
The path oscillates even more than in mini-batch.
--------------
-Nesterov accelerated gradient
✅ More responsive to the curvature of the loss surface. It is a lookhaed version of momentum

It calculates the gradient of the parameter ahead of the point you're at.
--------------
--------------
ADAPTIVE LEARNING RATE OPTIMIZERS
These optimizers adjust the learning rate for each parameter individually.

-adagrad
It helps correct direction of gradient towards the global optimum.
It decays the learning rate, this might be a problem if it would decrease it too much.
--------------
-RMSProp
Improves Adagrad(solves the early stopping problem) by using a decaying average of squared gradients:
--------------
-Adam
 (Adaptive Moment Estimation)
It is the Combination of momentum + RMSProp:
✅ Most popular optimizer in deep learning

✅ Works well out of the box

❌ Can sometimes generalize worse than SGD
--------------
 AdamW (Adam with Weight Decay)
Improves Adam by decoupling weight decay (L2 regularization):
--------------
- Nadam (Adam with nesterov trick), AMSGrad, AdaMax, etc.
Various enhancements on Adam and RMSProp to address convergence and stability in specific tasks.
--------------
Adam and AdamW are go-to optimizers for most deep learning tasks today.

SGD with momentum often gives better generalization in vision tasks (e.g., ResNet training).

RMSProp is still useful in training RNNs.

