🛡️ Regularization Techniques
Applying regularization techniques in neural networks helps prevent overfitting by constraining the model’s complexity. 
To prevent overfitting, deep learning uses several regularization strategies:

L1 Regularization: Adds the absolute value of weights to the loss. Encourages sparsity.

L2 Regularization (Weight Decay): Adds the squared weights to the loss. Penalizes large weights.

Dropout: Randomly “drops” a percentage of neurons during training. Forces the network to build redundant, robust representations.

⚠️ Vanishing & Exploding Gradients
In deep networks, gradients can either vanish (become too small to update weights) or explode (grow too large, causing instability).

Causes:
Poor initialization

Activation functions like sigmoid or tanh

Very deep networks

Solutions:
Use ReLU-based activations

Apply batch normalization

Use residual connections (ResNets)

Carefully initialize weights (e.g., He initialization)


Regularization consists in constraining a model to simplyify it:
L1/L2 reg, data augmentation, dropout

L1 e L2 are ways to keep a weight low.
What is the bond between weights and overfitting?

A weight can be thought as "the importance an input has".
If an input has a high importance it means that whatever comes out of  it should be considered as true.
When a model overfits it has high weights.

In a training loop we compute predictions based on the model,  than we calculate the error by the difference between the
prediction and the actual value. This error is used to compute the weights.
L1 and L2 add weights to the loss function, effectively penalizing the model for having high weights.

L1 regularization makes the network sparse. L1 encourages the weights to be 0, resulting in a more sparse network.
L1 adds the absolute values of the sum of the weights to the loss.

L2 regularization is also called weight decay
Add the sum of the squared values of the weights to the loss.

DROPOUT
at each step, a neuron has probability p of being inactive. p is the dropout rate.

EARLY STOPPING
You stop the training when the 2 curves: validation and training loss begin to differ, and you take the weights at the point of stopping.



