How to understand where the problem is and how we come up with a way 
to understand this problem?

WHen we are building machine learn. model, we must achieve a level 
at which the task is performed by the algorithm 
 way better than a human could do.
We have to define human-level performance.
Than, there is the Bayes optimal.
After human level performance is uncharted territory, where we 
can get close to bayes optimal, but it is difficult to achieve it 
and it can not get passed it. 

The difference  between human-level performance  and performance on the 
training set is the bias difference.
If the performance on tha training set is lower than the human-level 
performance it might mean that your model is not able to fit the training data
very well, it might UNDERFIT.
UNDERFITTING is caused by high bias.
If you are not able to fit tha data as you could have based on human-level performance 
that means that you should lower the bias.
If the performance on tha training set is good, but not on the validation set 
we call this variance difference. 
If you are performing well on the test set but not on the validation, 
you are overfitting. 
------------------------------------
human level performance       0.3 %
        |bias difference
Performance on training set    1%
        | variance difference 
Performance on validation set  4%

------------------------------------

Between the human-level performance and training set performance 
we have the bias difference.
Between the training set performance and the validation set performance 
we have the variance difference.
If we want to get closer to human-level performance,
 (aka lower the bias), we could train the model more, increase
 its complexity, tray more advance architectures and find better hyperparameters.

If we decide to close the gap between performance on the training and validation set.
 (lower variance) we could decide to introduce more data ,
add regularization, try different architecture because the current one 
is not able to understand the dataset very well or we could find better 
hyperparameters.

If there is a gap between validation and test sets we might have 
to have a bigger validation set, because that would point to us that 
validation set is not able to represent test set quite well.

If is not performing well in real life we might change the validation set 
or the cost function (the thing you are trying to get good 
performance is not the right thing ). 






 



------------------------------------------------

Early stopping and learning rate scheduling are optimization strategies that help your model generalize better and converge more efficiently. Here's why they improve results:

⛔ Early Stopping
🔍 What It Is
Early stopping monitors validation loss (or accuracy) during training. If it stops improving for a set number of epochs (patience), training halts.

✅ Why It Helps
Benefit	Explanation
Prevents overfitting	Stops training before the model memorizes noise in training data.
Saves compute time	Halts once the model reaches its best performance.
Keeps best weights	Typically stores and restores weights from the best epoch.

📈 Example
You're training for 100 epochs, but the validation loss stops improving after 12 epochs → Early stopping ends training there.

📉 Learning Rate Scheduling
🔍 What It Is
Learning rate scheduling adjusts the learning rate during training — often reducing it as the model starts converging.
LEARNING RATE SCHEDULING
How far in that direction?
-Manual approach: change it manually and choose a learning rate with a value a bit before when the loss starts shooting up.
-Power scheduling (learning rate is a function of the epoch number)
-exponential scheduling. learning rate is a function of the epoch number. it drops by a factor of 10 every epochs.
-piecewise constnat scheduling. use a constant scheduling rate for a certain number of epochs. very similar to manual.
-performance scheduling. Drops the learning rate when the validation error stops dropping.
-1 cycle scheduling. starting from a learning rate value, we slowly increase it and when a maximum value has been reached, the learning rate decreases again untill it reaches the starting value.


✅ Why It Helps
Benefit	Explanation
Faster convergence early on	High LR allows big, exploratory steps.
Fine-tuning at the end	Lower LR helps settle into a good minimum (avoids bouncing).
Avoids getting stuck	Dynamic LRs can escape plateaus or poor local minima.

🧪 Common Schedulers
StepLR: Reduce LR every n epochs.

ReduceLROnPlateau: Reduce LR when a metric stops improving.

CosineAnnealingLR: Smooth decay over time.

📘 PyTorch Example
python
Copy
Edit
from torch.optim.lr_scheduler import ReduceLROnPlateau

scheduler = ReduceLROnPlateau(optimizer, mode='min', patience=3, factor=0.5)
# In your training loop:
scheduler.step(validation_loss)
🧠 Summary
Strategy	Prevents...	Improves...
Early Stopping	Overfitting	Generalization
LR Scheduling	Poor convergence	Training efficiency & final accuracy

When combined, these techniques act like smart training brakes — pushing the model hard when it’s learning, and gently fine-tuning or stopping when it’s not. Essential for achieving optimal performance, especially with limited or noisy datasets.


